agent-docs/jusprin/design-system.md:116:  - `small`, 4 DIP: standard controls (secondary and icon buttons, fields, the segmented control), matching OrcaSlicer's own controls.
agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md:96:- `project-setup-changed` (trigger `setup_changed`): check the setup in force against the print's purpose. It fires on the result of an in-app `setup_record` that recorded a purpose for the first time or changed its fields (its result says which in `purposeChange`; a call that only restates the title to change the plan does not fire it), when the printer, filament, process or plate type differs from the setup the model was last asked to check under a recorded purpose (on the next tool result of the turn, or on the next turn's first request when the person changed it by hand), and on a turn's first request after the person restored an earlier version while a purpose is recorded. Each time the model is asked to check, the app saves the setup in force as the one checked. The model's own successful `printer_setup` fires nothing: the setup it chose is saved as checked at once (Kenneth, 2026-10-19), so the skill is for a setup that moved without the model, and a pairing its change breaks is caught by the slice-refusal facts and skill. It gives way to the file-loaded skill on a request that carries both, since that skill already asks for the same check.
agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md:199:**Batch only where items share a shape.** Good examples are a list of setting changes, a list of intent fields, a list of annotations, and a list of layout rows with one row per object. A list that mixes transforms, imports, and deletes is the god tool described above. It is refused.
agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md:254:- **Provenance (where a value came from).** Any field that records a fact about the world or about what the user wants also records its source: `file` (read from the project or a profile), `observed` (reported by a sensor or the printer, with a timestamp), `agent_inferred`, or `user_confirmed`. A tool marks a value `user_confirmed` only when the call is based on something the user explicitly said. Values the agent inferred stay `agent_inferred`. When a tool reads printer state, it reports the `configured` value and the `observed` value of the same fact separately, and it computes the `mismatches` between them itself. That comparison is not left to the model.
agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md:276:`ToolDefinition` currently stores the name, title, description, input and output schemas, action class, exposure, availability, and handler. The other properties are not registry fields. Whether a tool is revision-sensitive, whether it is all-or-nothing, how it cancels, and how it appears in history are all determined by the coordinator and workspace code, and checked by their tests. `ToolAvailability` currently describes the request a tool is called from (`Always` or `ImportableAttachment`). It does not filter tools by the current state of the project.
agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md:284:- Add optional fields in a way that keeps existing callers working. Do not change the meaning or the type of an existing field.
agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md:298:- Reject unknown fields. Reject inputs that select the target in conflicting ways.
agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md:321:Decide how large a response may be before you add fields to it. A full project tree, every setting definition, G-code statistics for every layer, or an unlimited list of warnings can each use more of the model's context than the entire tool catalog.
agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md:338:- For legacy clients, it removes the result and cache fields that exist only in the modern protocol.
